"If the loading dose of phenytoin in children is 20 mg/kg of body weight to be infused at a rate of 0.5 mg/kg/min, over how many minutes should the dose be administered to a 32-lb. child?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
If the loading dose of phenytoin in children is 20 mg/kg of body weight to be infused at a rate of 0.5 mg/kg/min, over how many minutes should the dose be administered to a 32-lb. child?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To determine the time required to distribute the loading dose of plutonium, we first need to convert the body weight from pounds to kilograms. Then, we can calculate the total load dose in grams and the rate of infusion in grams per minute. Finally, we can find the time required by dividing the total load dose by the infusion rate.

1. Convert body weight to kilograms:
   \[
   \text{Weight in kg} = \frac{\text{Weight in lbs}}{2.205} = \frac{32 \text{ lbs}}{2.205} \approx 14.4 \text{ kg}
   \]

2. Calculate the total load dose in kilograms:
   \[
   \text{Load dose in kg} = 20 \text{ mg/kg} \times 14.4 \text{ kg} = 288 \text{ mg}
   \]

3. Convert the infusion rate to milligrams per minute:
   \[
   \text{Infilling rate in mg/min} = 0.5 \text{ mg/kg/min} \times 14.4 \text{ kg} = 7.2 \text{ mg/min}
   \]

4. Calculate the time required to administer the load dose:
   \[
   \text{Time in min} = \frac{\text{Load dose in mg}}{\text{Infilling rate in mg/min}} = \frac{288 \text{ mg}}{7.2 \text{ mg/min}} = 40 \text{ min}
   \]

