What is absolute asymmetric synthesis, and how does it differ from other types of asymmetric synthesis? Provide an example to illustrate this concept.
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
Absolute asymmetric synthesis refers to the preparation of optically active compounds from achiral (optically inactive) starting materials without the use of any chiral reagent, catalyst, or auxiliary. Instead, it relies on a physically dissymmetric influence, such as circularly polarized light, to induce chirality in the product. This differs from other types of asymmetric synthesis, which typically require a chiral agent (e.g., a chiral catalyst or substrate) to achieve enantioselectivity.  

For example, when bromine is added to 2,4,6-trinitrostilbene under irradiation with right-circularly polarized light, the reaction yields a dextrorotatory dibromo product. Here, the dissymmetric influence of the polarized light drives the formation of one enantiomer preferentially, demonstrating absolute asymmetric synthesis.
</GROUND_TRUTH>

