","Solution:

$(1)$ Since the parabola $C$ is $y^{2}=2px (p > 0)$,

it has focus $F(p,0)$, and its focus distance $a=p$.

Since the point $(3,m)$ is on the parabola $C$,

we have $m^{2}=2p \cdot 3$,

thus $m^{2}=6p$,

which implies $p= \frac{m^{2}}{6}$.

Since the distance from the point $(3,m)$ to the focus $F(p,0)$ is $5$,

we have $\sqrt{(3-p)^{2}+m^{2}}=5$,

which implies $(3-p)^{2}+m^{2}=25$,

which implies $(3-p)^{2}+6p=25$,

which implies $9-6p+p^{2}+6p=25$,

which implies $p^{2}=16$,

thus $p=4$,

thus the equation of the parabola $C$ is $y^{2}=8x$.

$(2)$ Let the equation of line $l$ be $y=kx-4k$,

thus the intersection point of line $l$ and the parabola $C$ satisfies the system of equations $y^{2}=8x$ and $y=kx-4k$,

which gives $k^{2}(x-4k)^{2}=8x$,

which implies $k^{2}(x^{2}-8kx+16k^{2})=8x$,

which implies $k^{2}x^{2}-8k^{3}x+16k^{3}=8x$,

