Therefore, $\cos (\frac{\omega \pi}{2}+\varphi)=-\sin \varphi=\cos (\frac{\pi}{2}+\varphi)$.

This implies $\frac{\omega \pi}{2}+\varphi=\pm (\frac{\pi}{2}+\varphi)+2k\pi$, where $k\in\mathbb{Z}$.

Solving these equations, we get two possible forms:

1. $\frac{\omega \pi}{2}+\varphi=\frac{\pi}{2}+\varphi+2k\pi$, which simplifies to $\omega=1$, and $k\in\mathbb{Z}$.

2. $\frac{\omega \pi}{2}+\varphi=-\frac{\pi}{2}-\varphi+2k\pi$, which simplifies to $\omega=-1$, and $k\in\mathbb{Z}$.

