B = (2 * π * I) / (2 * π * R)

where:
B is the magnetic force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force force field (in Newtons per Ampere-meter)
I is the current (in Amperes)
R is the radius of the coil (in meters)

In this case, we have:
I = 1 A
R = 0.2 m
n = 100 (number of turns)

The formula for the magnetic field at the center of a circular coil with N turns is:

B = (n * 2 * π * I) / (2 * π * R)

Simplifying the expression, we get:

B = (n * I) / R

Now, we can plug in the values:

B = (100 * 1) / 0.2

B = 100 / 0.2

B = 500 N/A⋅m

So, the magnetic field strength at the center of the circular coil is 500 N/A⋅m.",relevance,"# Task Description
