<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the work done by the gas under an isothermal expansion, we can use the formula for the work done in an elastic elastic system:

\[
W = -k(V_f - V_i)
\]

For an ideal gas, the relationship between pressure and volume in an inelastic elastic system is given by the Beer's law:

\[
PV = k
\]

where \( k \) is a constant.

Given the initial conditions, we can find the constant \( k \):

\[
k = P_i V_i = 2 \, \text{At} \times 4 \, \text{L} = 8 \, \text{At} \cdot \text{L}
\]

The final volume \( V_f = 8 \, \text{L} \), and the initial volume \( V_i = 4 \, \text{L} \).

Now, we can calculate the work done:

\[
W = -k(V_f - V_i) = -8 \, \text{At} \cdot \text{L} \cdot (8 \, \text{L} - 4 \, \text{L}) = -8 \, \text{At} \cdot \text{L} \cdot 4 \, \text{L} = -32 \, \text{At} \cdot \text{L}
\]

The work done is \(-32 \, \text{At} \cdot \text{L}\). To convert this to Joules, we use the conversion factor \( 1 \, \text{At} \cdot \text{L} = 10^9 \, \text{J} \):

\[
W = -32 \, \text{At} \cdot \text{L} \times 10^9 \, \text{J} / (\text{At} \cdot \text{L}) = -32 \times 10^9 \, \text{J} = -32 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the work done by the gas during the expansion process is \(\boxed{-32 \, \text{J}}\).
