","To find the area of the isosceles triangle, we can use the formula for the area of a triangle:
\[ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} \]

First, we need to find the height of the triangle. Since the triangle is isosceles, the height from the base to the midpoint of the base will divide the triangle into two right triangles. The base of each right triangle is half of the base of the isosceles triangle, which is \( \frac{20}{2} = 10 \) units. The hypotenuse of each right triangle is one of the equal sides of the isosceles triangle, which is 15 units.

Using the Pythagorean theorem in one of the right triangles:
\[ \text{leg}^2 + \text{leg}^2 = \text{hypotenuse}^2 \]
\[ 10^2 + h^2 = 15^2 \]
\[ 100 + h^2 = 225 \]
\[ h^2 = 225 - 100 \]
\[ h^2 = 125 \]
\[ h = \sqrt{125} \]
\[ h = 5\sqrt{5} \]

Now that we have the height \( h = 5\sqrt{5} \), we can calculate the area of the isosceles triangle:
\[ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} \]
\[ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times 20 \times 5\sqrt{5} \]
\[ \text{Area} = 10 \times 5\sqrt{5} \]
\[ \text{Area} = 50\sqrt{5} \]

So, the area of the isosceles triangle is \(\boxed{50\sqrt{5}}\) square units.",relevance,"# Task Description
