","The forward voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \). The feedback fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \). The gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to aging. The closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf} \) is given by:

\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v}
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
A_{vf} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} \approx 111.11 \text{ V^{-1}}
\]

The gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB, which means the new forward gain \( A_v' \) is:

\[
A_v' = \frac{A_v}{2} = \frac{100}{2} = 50 \text{ V^{-1}}
\]

The new closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf}' \) is:

\[
A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 - m_v} = \frac{50}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{50}{0.9} \approx 55.56 \text{ V^{-1}}
\]

The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:

\[
\text{Percentage Change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100\% = \left( \frac{55.56 - 111.11}{111.11} \right) \times 100\% = \left( \frac{-55.55}{111.11} \right) \times 100\% \approx -50\%
\]

